course: dictionary
lesson: 10107
title: Thinning: getting less or thinner
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Thinning means becoming thinner or less thick, or getting smaller in number. The word comes from thin, so if you know thin, you are already close to the meaning.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Grandad's hair is thinning» tells you his hair is getting less, not that it is turning into a thin line. Always check by swapping in "getting thinner": if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: One word can mean different things in different places. In a garden, thinning means pulling up some young plants so the others have room to grow. In cooking or painting, thinning means adding water to make something runnier. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning to use.
fact: Thinning means becoming thinner or less thick.
fact: «Thinning on top» is a polite way to say going bald.
fact: Thinning out in a garden means pulling up some young plants so the rest have space, light and water.
fact: A crowd or traffic that is thinning is getting smaller.
fact: Thinning is the present participle of the verb thin: thin, thins, thinned, thinning.
exercise: «Grandad's hair is thinning.» What does thinning mean here? | Growing longer and curlier | *Getting less and thinner | Falling out all in one day | Turning a darker colour
exercise: Mia pulls up some young plants so the others have room to grow. What is she doing? | Painting the fence | Watering the lawn | Mixing the soil into a thick paste | *Thinning out the seedlings
exercise: The crowd at the match is thinning as the game ends. What does thinning mean? | Getting hotter | *Getting smaller | Getting louder | Getting taller
test: Dad says, "Add a little water to thin the paint." What does thin mean here? | *To make it runnier | To make it stronger | To make it shiny | To make it thicker
test: «The ice in the Arctic is thinning.» What does this tell you? | The ice is growing more thick each year | The ice is getting taller | *The ice is getting less thick | The ice is turning into a crowd
exam: «The ozone layer is thinning.» Which meaning fits best here? | Glowing brightly like a lamp | Staying exactly the same | *Getting thinner, with less protection | Getting thicker, with more protection
